     Real-Life Example

     Imagine two people are using the same bank account.

     Balance = ₹1000

     Both try to withdraw ₹500 at the same time.

     Thread 1
     Reads balance = ₹1000
     Calculates new balance = ₹500
     Thread 2
     Also reads balance = ₹1000
     Calculates new balance = ₹500

     Now both write ₹500.

     Final Balance:

     ₹500

     But it should have been:

     ₹0

     This happened because both threads raced to update the same data.

     This is called a Race Condition.

     Java Example (Without Synchronization)
     class Counter {
         int count = 0;

         void increment() {
             count++;
         }
     }

     public class Main {
         public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

             Counter c = new Counter();

             Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
                 for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++)
                     c.increment();
             });

             Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
                 for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++)
                     c.increment();
             });

             t1.start();
             t2.start();

             t1.join();
             t2.join();

             System.out.println(c.count);
         }
     }
     Expected Output
     200000
     Actual Output (may vary)
     183456

     or

     197842

     or

     199999

     The output changes every run because of a race condition.

     Why does this happen?

     Many beginners think:

     count++;

     is one operation.

     It is not.

     Internally it is approximately:

     int temp = count;   // Read
     temp = temp + 1;    // Modify
     count = temp;       // Write

     Suppose:

     Initial value

     count = 5
     Thread 1

     Reads

     5
     Thread 2

     Also reads

     5

     Thread 1 writes

     6

     Thread 2 also writes

     6

     Expected:

     7

     Actual:

     6

     One increment is lost.


